In the given function, $b$ is a constant. If $g(c + 7) = \frac{c}{4}$, where $c$ is a positive constant, what is the value of $b$?', NULL, '{"A":"$b = \\frac{61c}{4} + 105$","B":"$b = \\frac{c}{4} + 105$","C":"$b = -\\frac{59c}{4} + 105$","D":"$b = \\frac{c}{4} - 105$"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, 'MCQ', NULL, '{"corpus":"official-cb","file":"linear-functions.ndjson"}'::jsonb);
INSERT INTO public.cb_questions (id, section, domain, cb_skill, difficulty, stem, passage, options, correct_answer, explanation, item_type, assets, source_ref)
VALUES ('023c0a8d', 'M', 'Algebra', 'Linear Functions', 'Hard', 'For the linear function $f$, the equation $f(3x) = x - 6$ is true for all values of $x$. What is the value of $f(6)$?', NULL, '{"A":"$-6$","B":"$-4$","C":"0","D":"2"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, 'MCQ', NULL, '{"corpus":"official-cb","file":"linear-functions.ndjson"}'::jsonb);
INSERT INTO public.cb_questions (id, section, domain, cb_skill, difficulty, stem, passage, options, correct_answer, explanation, item_type, assets, source_ref)
VALUES ('daad7c32', 'M', 'Algebra', 'Linear Functions', 'Hard', 'A spring hangs from the ceiling. The equation $\ell = 30 + 2w$ represents the length $\ell$, in centimeters, of the spring when a weight of $w$ newtons is attached to the end of the spring. What is the best interpretation of 2 in this context?', NULL, '{"A":"The length, in centimeters, of the spring with no weight attached.","B":"The weight, in newtons, attached to the spring when the spring is 30 centimeters long.","C":"The increase in the weight, in newtons, attached to the spring for each one centimeter increase in length.","D":"The increase in the length, in centimeters, of the spring for each one newton increase in the weight attached."}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, 'MCQ', NULL, '{"corpus":"official-cb","file":"linear-functions.ndjson"}'::jsonb);
INSERT INTO public.cb_questions (id, section, domain, cb_skill, difficulty, stem, passage, options, correct_answer, explanation, item_type, assets, source_ref)
VALUES ('e103300e', 'M', 'Algebra', 'Linear Functions', 'Hard', 'The function $f(x)$ is defined as 19 more than 4 times $x$. What is the best interpretation of the x-intercept of the graph of $y = f(x)$ in the $xy$-plane?', NULL, '{"A":"When $f(x) = 0$, the value of $x$ is $-\\frac{19}{4}$.","B":"When the value of $x$ is 0, $f(x) = 19$.","C":"For every increase of $x$ by 1, the value of $f(x)$ increases by 4.","D":"For every increase of $x$ by 4, the value of $f(x)$ increases by 1."}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, 'MCQ', NULL, '{"corpus":"official-cb","file":"linear-functions.ndjson"}'::jsonb);
INSERT INTO public.cb_questions (id, section, domain, cb_skill, difficulty, stem, passage, options, correct_answer, explanation, item_type, assets, source_ref)
VALUES ('e86a06fe', 'M', 'Algebra', 'Linear Functions', 'Hard', 'Kaylani makes custom suits. The equation $y - 5x = 6$ represents the relationship between the number of yards of fabric, $y$, used and the number of suits made, $x$. What is the best interpretation of 6 in this equation?', NULL, '{"A":"6 yards of fabric is the fixed amount of fabric used regardless of the number of suits.","B":"Each suit uses 6 yards of fabric.","C":"Kaylani makes 6 suits.","D":"Kaylani uses 6 yards of fabric per suit."}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, 'MCQ', NULL, '{"corpus":"official-cb","file":"linear-functions.ndjson"}'::jsonb);
